{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_plzzlavsx7omuhoxofgqzjqw3tzekokolneubiddpun3ck7w2drq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, independently verified.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 83-line file exporting 5 symbols: `driver(int)` dispatches to `good()` (correct `alloca(10*sizeof(int))`) or `bad()` (CWE-806: `alloca(10)` under-allocation, then writes 40 bytes), plus `printLine`/`printIntLine` stdio helpers.\n\n## Phase A artifacts\n\n`SYMBOLS.md` (5 symbols, 0 missing), `ERRORS.md` (10 rows — the C has exactly **one** input guard, `line != NULL`; no error codes, asserts, or enums anywhere), `CONFIGS.md` (26 rows across the 5 entry points × payload/value/length/multiplicity axes).\n\n## Two real bugs found — both in my test harness, not the translation\n\nThe translation itself was correct; the verification was not. Mutation testing caught this:\n\n1. **The suite was testing stale code.** `cargo test` builds test binaries but *not* the `cdylib`, so the harness was `dlopen`ing a `libdriver.so` from an earlier build. My first mutation run showed **all 5 injected bugs passing** — the tests were verifying nothing. Fixed by always rebuilding + asserting the `.so` is newer than `src/lib.rs`.\n2. **libtest's progress output was corrupting captures.** Parallel test threads wrote `test cfg_… ok` into my fd-1 capture windows, producing 4 fake \"divergences.\" Fixed by pinning `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1`, asserting it at capture time, and flushing Rust's `stdout` before each redirect.\n\nHad I stopped at \"39 tests pass,\" I'd have reported a verified translation on the strength of a suite that couldn't fail.\n\n## Mutation evidence (`MUTATION.md`)\n\n11 of 14 mutants caught. The 3 escapees are **provably equivalent**, not gaps — I verified against the C `.so` that `good()` and `bad()` emit byte-identical output (`0\\n`), so `driver`'s branch is *unobservable* through the public API. No stdout-based test can distinguish it. I documented this rather than papering over it, verified the predicate by source correspondence (`if (useGood)` ↔ `!= 0`), and pinned the equivalence in a test that will start failing the moment those two functions diverge.\n\n## Results\n\n43 tests across 3 files, all passing under 6 configurations (3 feature combos × debug/release). Symbol diff is empty. `panic = \"abort\"` release builds included, so any panic would have aborted.\n\nTwo caveats worth flagging:\n\n- **`driver`'s dispatch is not differentially verified**, because it cannot be. This is the one behavioral claim resting on source inspection rather than observed output.\n- **`bad()`'s C behavior is UB.** The C writes 40 bytes into a 10-byte `alloca`; the surplus lands in stack slack. It printed `0` across every invocation I tested (500+ consecutive, plus interleaved with other calls), and the Rust matches — but \"matches the C as observed on this compiler/platform\" is the strongest available guarantee for an out-of-bounds write. A different compiler or stack layout could in principle differ.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified; only `c_src/build/` was added, as the task's build instructions required."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1002,"cache_read_input_tokens":97448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1002,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3bb16385-bb18-4519-b39a-41db6c5306ea","uuid":"ebd82516-d18e-4c8f-90ef-50ec5d657523","timestamp":"2026-08-28T13:25:04.211Z"}
